      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Part 1 - Process States",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Part 1 - Process States",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FoHa4xLis7uxXO4nZpOGM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 805.2898660056643,
      "y": 6047.56709422141,
      "width": 1133,
      "height": 2725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b43",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1470900982,
      "version": 380,
      "versionNonce": 91138550,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785507716885,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Yesterday we learned: A process is a running program.\nBut is every process always using the CPU? -> No\n\nSuppose you open Chrome.\nYou don't touch it for 10 minutes.\nIs Chrome still running? -> Yes\nIs it continuously using the CPU?  -> No\n\nA process changes state throughout its lifetime.\n\nLinux Process Lifecycle\n\n            +---------+\n            |  New    |\n            +---------+\n                 │\n                 ▼\n          +--------------+\n          |   Runnable   |\n          +--------------+\n                 │\n          CPU Scheduler\n                 │\n        ┌────────┴────────┐\n        ▼                 ▼\n +---------------+   +------------+\n |    Running    |<->|  Runnable  |\n +---------------+   +------------+\n        │\n        ├───────────────┐\n        ▼               ▼\n +-------------+   +------------+\n |  Sleeping   |   |  Stopped   |\n +-------------+   +------------+\n        │\n        ▼\n +-------------+\n | Running     |\n +-------------+\n        │\n        ▼\n +-------------+\n | Terminated  |\n +-------------+\n\n\nProcess States You'll See\n    RUN -> ps -el -> it will show all the process states\n\nYou'll see process states like:\n    R, S, D, T, Z\n\n\n1. R → Running / Runnable\n    Example:\n        while True:\n            pass\n    This process continuously uses the CPU.\n    State: -> R\n    Meaning:\n\n        Running right now or\n        Ready to run when the scheduler gives it CPU time.\n\n2. S → Sleeping (Most Common)\n    Example:\n        import time\n        time.sleep(60)\n        The process isn't doing work. It's waiting.\n        State -> S\n\n    Most processes on Linux are sleeping.\n    Examples:\n\n    PostgreSQL waiting for queries.\n    Nginx waiting for requests.\n    Django waiting for API calls.\n    \n    This is completely normal.\n\n\n3. D → Uninterruptible Sleep\n\n    This one is interesting. Imagine PostgreSQL is reading data from disk.\n    Disk I/O takes time.\n    While waiting, Linux puts the process into: state D\n\n    Why \"Uninterruptible\"?\n        Because interrupting a process in the middle of critical disk I/O could leave data in an \n        inconsistent state.\n\n    Interview Question\n    Can kill -9 always kill a process in state D?\n    \n    No.A process in uninterruptible sleep is waiting for the kernel to complete certain operations \n    (often I/O). The signal is noted, but the process typically won't exit until it leaves the D state.\n\n    Many developers incorrectly believe kill -9 is absolutely unstoppable.\n\n\n4. T → Stopped\n    Example: Ctrl + Z\n    The process pauses. Not terminated. Just stopped.\n\n    For Resume RUN:\n        fg or bg\n\n5. Z → Zombie\n    One of the most famous Linux interview questions. Let's understand why zombies exist.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Yesterday we learned: A process is a running program.\nBut is every process always using the CPU? -> No\n\nSuppose you open Chrome.\nYou don't touch it for 10 minutes.\nIs Chrome still running? -> Yes\nIs it continuously using the CPU?  -> No\n\nA process changes state throughout its lifetime.\n\nLinux Process Lifecycle\n\n            +---------+\n            |  New    |\n            +---------+\n                 │\n                 ▼\n          +--------------+\n          |   Runnable   |\n          +--------------+\n                 │\n          CPU Scheduler\n                 │\n        ┌────────┴────────┐\n        ▼                 ▼\n +---------------+   +------------+\n |    Running    |<->|  Runnable  |\n +---------------+   +------------+\n        │\n        ├───────────────┐\n        ▼               ▼\n +-------------+   +------------+\n |  Sleeping   |   |  Stopped   |\n +-------------+   +------------+\n        │\n        ▼\n +-------------+\n | Running     |\n +-------------+\n        │\n        ▼\n +-------------+\n | Terminated  |\n +-------------+\n\n\nProcess States You'll See\n    RUN -> ps -el -> it will show all the process states\n\nYou'll see process states like:\n    R, S, D, T, Z\n\n\n1. R → Running / Runnable\n    Example:\n        while True:\n            pass\n    This process continuously uses the CPU.\n    State: -> R\n    Meaning:\n\n        Running right now or\n        Ready to run when the scheduler gives it CPU time.\n\n2. S → Sleeping (Most Common)\n    Example:\n        import time\n        time.sleep(60)\n        The process isn't doing work. It's waiting.\n        State -> S\n\n    Most processes on Linux are sleeping.\n    Examples:\n\n    PostgreSQL waiting for queries.\n    Nginx waiting for requests.\n    Django waiting for API calls.\n    \n    This is completely normal.\n\n\n3. D → Uninterruptible Sleep\n\n    This one is interesting. Imagine PostgreSQL is reading data from disk.\n    Disk I/O takes time.\n    While waiting, Linux puts the process into: state D\n\n    Why \"Uninterruptible\"?\n        Because interrupting a process in the middle of critical disk I/O could leave data in an \n        inconsistent state.\n\n    Interview Question\n    Can kill -9 always kill a process in state D?\n    \n    No.A process in uninterruptible sleep is waiting for the kernel to complete certain operations \n    (often I/O). The signal is noted, but the process typically won't exit until it leaves the D state.\n\n    Many developers incorrectly believe kill -9 is absolutely unstoppable.\n\n\n4. T → Stopped\n    Example: Ctrl + Z\n    The process pauses. Not terminated. Just stopped.\n\n    For Resume RUN:\n        fg or bg\n\n5. Z → Zombie\n    One of the most famous Linux interview questions. Let's understand why zombies exist.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "o3t8zZ6Wd2ABeYZO-PuCj",
