      "text": "Part 3 - fork()",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Part 3 - fork()",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "mPzpmj4Vxp9QfFIlk1bet",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 775.0974317951373,
      "y": 10172.599988958242,
      "width": 1738,
      "height": 1300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 299021610,
      "version": 337,
      "versionNonce": 2726966,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785508594041,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This is one of the most important Linux concepts.\n\nQuestion: When you type: python app.py, Who creates the Python process?\nAnswer: The shell. How? Using:fork()\n\nWhat Does fork() Do?\n    imagine bash call fork()\n\n    Now there are two processes.\n    bash (Parent) -> bash(child copy)\n\nnitially, the child is almost an identical copy of the parent (modern kernels optimize this with copy-on-write, so memory isn't fully duplicated immediately).\nSame memory layout.\nSame environment variables.\nSame working directory.\nDifferent PID.\n\nWhy Make a Copy?\n    Because the parent (your shell) should continue running after starting another program.\nIf Bash simply replaced itself with Python every time, you'd lose your shell after each command.\nInstead:\nBash creates a child with fork().\nThe child becomes Python.\nThe parent Bash waits for the child to finish and then shows the prompt again.\n\nexec()\n\nNow the child process is still Bash.\nWe don't want another Bash.\nWe want Python.\nThe child calls:\n    execve()\n\nexecve() replaces the child process's program image with the Python executable.\n\nAfter execve():\n\n    bash -> fork() -> child bash -> exec() -> Python\n\nThe PID stays the same, but the program running inside that process changes.\n\nWhy fork() + exec()?\n    Because we want:\n    Parent Bash alive.\n    Child becomes Python.\n\nWithout fork():\n    bash -> exec() -> python\n    Your shell would disappear.\n\nComplete Flow\n    Terminal -> bash -> fork() -> child bash -> exec(Python) -> Python Reads app.py -> Python Executes Code",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This is one of the most important Linux concepts.\n\nQuestion: When you type: python app.py, Who creates the Python process?\nAnswer: The shell. How? Using:fork()\n\nWhat Does fork() Do?\n    imagine bash call fork()\n\n    Now there are two processes.\n    bash (Parent) -> bash(child copy)\n\nnitially, the child is almost an identical copy of the parent (modern kernels optimize this with copy-on-write, so memory isn't fully duplicated immediately).\nSame memory layout.\nSame environment variables.\nSame working directory.\nDifferent PID.\n\nWhy Make a Copy?\n    Because the parent (your shell) should continue running after starting another program.\nIf Bash simply replaced itself with Python every time, you'd lose your shell after each command.\nInstead:\nBash creates a child with fork().\nThe child becomes Python.\nThe parent Bash waits for the child to finish and then shows the prompt again.\n\nexec()\n\nNow the child process is still Bash.\nWe don't want another Bash.\nWe want Python.\nThe child calls:\n    execve()\n\nexecve() replaces the child process's program image with the Python executable.\n\nAfter execve():\n\n    bash -> fork() -> child bash -> exec() -> Python\n\nThe PID stays the same, but the program running inside that process changes.\n\nWhy fork() + exec()?\n    Because we want:\n    Parent Bash alive.\n    Child becomes Python.\n\nWithout fork():\n    bash -> exec() -> python\n    Your shell would disappear.\n\nComplete Flow\n    Terminal -> bash -> fork() -> child bash -> exec(Python) -> Python Reads app.py -> Python Executes Code",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
