    {
      "id": "ZMjsgrDGdMnLBAFjHQAix",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 758.3153594267159,
      "y": 11549.97663369507,
      "width": 1089,
      "height": 1400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4F",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 206804214,
      "version": 224,
      "versionNonce": 687064618,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785508896003,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Suppose your program is running.\nHow do we communicate with it?\nSignals.\nSignals are asynchronous notifications sent by the kernel or another process.\n\nSIGINT\n    Ctrl + C\nMeaning:\nPlease interrupt.Many programs catch this signal and shut down gracefully.\n\nSIGTERM\n    kill PID\n    Default signal.\n    Meaning:\n        Please terminate gracefully.\n        The process can clean up resources before exiting.\n    This is what Docker and Kubernetes generally send first when stopping a container.\n\nSIGKILL\n    kill -9 PID\n    Meaning:\n            Stop immediately.\n    The kernel terminates the process without giving it a chance to clean up.\n    The process cannot ignore or handle SIGKILL.\n\n\nWhy is kill -9 Dangerous?\n    Imagine PostgreSQL.\n    It is writing:\n        Money Transfer -> Disk\n    You send:\n        kill -9 postgres\n\n    The process dies immediately.It doesn't get a chance to finish cleanup or shut down gracefully.\n     That's why SIGTERM is preferred first.\n\n\n\nDocker Connection\n    docker stop container\n    Docker first sends:\n        SIGTERM\n        If the process doesn't exit within the configured timeout:\n        Docker sends:\n            SIGKILL\n\n\nKubernetes Connection\nKubernetes behaves similarly:\n    SIGTERM -> wait(grace period) -> SIGKILL\n\nThis gives your application time to:\n    Finish requests.\n    Close database connections.\n    Flush logs.\n    Save state if necessary.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Suppose your program is running.\nHow do we communicate with it?\nSignals.\nSignals are asynchronous notifications sent by the kernel or another process.\n\nSIGINT\n    Ctrl + C\nMeaning:\nPlease interrupt.Many programs catch this signal and shut down gracefully.\n\nSIGTERM\n    kill PID\n    Default signal.\n    Meaning:\n        Please terminate gracefully.\n        The process can clean up resources before exiting.\n    This is what Docker and Kubernetes generally send first when stopping a container.\n\nSIGKILL\n    kill -9 PID\n    Meaning:\n            Stop immediately.\n    The kernel terminates the process without giving it a chance to clean up.\n    The process cannot ignore or handle SIGKILL.\n\n\nWhy is kill -9 Dangerous?\n    Imagine PostgreSQL.\n    It is writing:\n        Money Transfer -> Disk\n    You send:\n        kill -9 postgres\n\n    The process dies immediately.It doesn't get a chance to finish cleanup or shut down gracefully.\n     That's why SIGTERM is preferred first.\n\n\n\nDocker Connection\n    docker stop container\n    Docker first sends:\n        SIGTERM\n        If the process doesn't exit within the configured timeout:\n        Docker sends:\n            SIGKILL\n\n\nKubernetes Connection\nKubernetes behaves similarly:\n    SIGTERM -> wait(grace period) -> SIGKILL\n\nThis gives your application time to:\n    Finish requests.\n    Close database connections.\n    Flush logs.\n    Save state if necessary.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
