      "updated": 1785509999148,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ps -> only shows current terminal's processes\nps -ef -> entire linux system\n    -e → Show every process on the system.\n    -f → Show the full format (more details).\n\nExample Output\n\n    UID        PID  PPID  C STIME TTY          TIME CMD\n    root         1     0  0 10:00 ?        00:00:03 /usr/lib/systemd/systemd\n    root       701     1  0 10:01 ?        00:00:00 sshd\n    amit      2501   701  0 10:15 pts/0    00:00:00 bash\n    amit      2610  2501  0 10:20 pts/0    00:00:02 python app.py\n    postgres   890     1  0 10:00 ?        00:00:05 postgres\n\nUID ->  Linux administrator started it.\nPID ->  Process ID.\nPPID -> Parent Process ID\nC -> CPU usage (historically the scheduling priority indicator; on many systems it \n    reflects recent CPU utilization). usually 0 , because most process are die\n\nSTIME -> Start time\nTTY -> Terminal associated with the process.\n    pts/0 -> means started from Terminal 0\n    ? -> No interactive Terminal\n    Background services like:\n\n    Docker\n    PostgreSQL\n    Nginx\n        usually shows ?\n\nTIME -> CPU time consumed by the process. 00:00:03 -> \n    The process has actually used 3 seconds of CPU time.\n    Even if it has been running for hours, it may have spent most of that time sleeping.\n\nCMD -> The command that started the process.\n     Example -> python app.py, postgres, systemd",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ps -> only shows current terminal's processes\nps -ef -> entire linux system\n    -e → Show every process on the system.\n    -f → Show the full format (more details).\n\nExample Output\n\n    UID        PID  PPID  C STIME TTY          TIME CMD\n    root         1     0  0 10:00 ?        00:00:03 /usr/lib/systemd/systemd\n    root       701     1  0 10:01 ?        00:00:00 sshd\n    amit      2501   701  0 10:15 pts/0    00:00:00 bash\n    amit      2610  2501  0 10:20 pts/0    00:00:02 python app.py\n    postgres   890     1  0 10:00 ?        00:00:05 postgres\n\nUID ->  Linux administrator started it.\nPID ->  Process ID.\nPPID -> Parent Process ID\nC -> CPU usage (historically the scheduling priority indicator; on many systems it \n    reflects recent CPU utilization). usually 0 , because most process are die\n\nSTIME -> Start time\nTTY -> Terminal associated with the process.\n    pts/0 -> means started from Terminal 0\n    ? -> No interactive Terminal\n    Background services like:\n\n    Docker\n    PostgreSQL\n    Nginx\n        usually shows ?\n\nTIME -> CPU time consumed by the process. 00:00:03 -> \n    The process has actually used 3 seconds of CPU time.\n    Even if it has been running for hours, it may have spent most of that time sleeping.\n\nCMD -> The command that started the process.\n     Example -> python app.py, postgres, systemd",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
